Power of a discrete-time signal is \[P=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{2N+1}\sum_{n=-N}^{N}|x(n)|^2\] <strong>Option (A):</strong> \(x(n)=u(n)\) \[P=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{2N+1}
\sum_{n=0}^{N}1
=
\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{N+1}{2N+1}
=
\frac{1}{2}\] Hence, unit step sequence is a power signal. <strong>Option (B):</strong> \(x(n)=e^{j\omega_0 n}\) \[P=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{2N+1}
\sum_{n=-N}^{N}|e^{j\omega_0 n}|^2\] \[=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{2N+1}
\sum_{n=-N}^{N}1
=
1\] Hence, \(e^{j\omega_0 n}\) is a power signal. <strong>Option (C):</strong> A periodic sequence. Every periodic sequence has finite average power. Hence, it is a power signal. <strong>Option (D):</strong> \(x(n)=nu(n)\) \[P=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{2N+1}
\sum_{n=0}^{N}n^2\] \[=\lim_{N\to\infty}
\frac{N(N+1)(2N+1)}
{6(2N+1)}\] \[=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{N(N+1)}{6}
=\infty\] Thus, the unit ramp sequence is not a power signal. (D) Unit ramp sequence

At DC, both capacitors behave as open circuits. Hence, the signal sources are disconnected from the op-amp. The inverting input is connected only through the feedback resistor, while the non-inverting input is at ground through the \(10R\) resistor. Therefore, the circuit behaves as a voltage follower for the input offset voltage. The output DC error due to the input offset voltage is \[V_{o(\text{error})}
=
\left(1+\frac{R_f}{R_{in}}\right)V_{ios}
=
1\times V_{ios},\] since the input path is open (\(R_{in}\rightarrow\infty\)). Given, \[V_{ios}=2\,\text{mV},\] therefore, \[V_{o(\text{error})}=2\,\text{mV}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }2\,\text{mV}}\]
